#{[17592186060935 :tutorial/content "Remember that we can represent a system of two linear equations in two variables using matrices. We can do the same thing for a system of three linear equations in three variables, such as the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\color{blue}{3}}x+{\\color{blue}{2}}y\\text{ }{\\color{blue}{-}}\\text{ }{\\color{blue}{2}}z &={\\color{red}{7}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{2}}x\\text{ }{\\color{blue}{-}}\\text{ }{\\color{blue}{1}}y\\text{ }{\\color{blue}{-}}\\text{ }{\\color{blue}{4}}z &={\\color{red}{2}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{2}}x+{\\color{blue}{1}}y\\text{ }{\\color{blue}{-}}\\text{ }{\\color{blue}{3}}z &={\\color{red}{3}}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFor a $3 \\times 3$ system like the one above, we put the coefficients of the system into a $3 \\times 3$ coefficient matrix:\n\n\n\n$$\nA= \\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{blue}3} & {\\color{blue}2} & {\\color{blue}-2} \\\\ {\\color{blue}2} & {\\color{blue}-1} & {\\color{blue}-4} \\\\ {\\color{blue}2} & {\\color{blue}1} & {\\color{blue}-3}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n\n\nEach row has the coefficients of one of the equations in the system, and each column consists of the coefficients of one particular variable.\nWe also have the $3 \\times 1$ column vectors\n\n\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{b} = \\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{red}7} \\\\ {\\color{red}2} \\\\ {\\color{red}3}\\end{bmatrix} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\mathbf{x} = \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{b}$ consists of each constant on the right side of the system and $\\mathbf{x}$ contains the variables.\nWith these matrices, we can write our system as $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b},$ or\n\n\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{blue}3} & {\\color{blue}2} & {\\color{blue}-2} \\\\ {\\color{blue}2} & {\\color{blue}-1} & {\\color{blue}-4} \\\\ {\\color{blue}2} & {\\color{blue}1} & {\\color{blue}-3}\\end{bmatrix} \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{red}7} \\\\ {\\color{red}2} \\\\ {\\color{red}3}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n\n\nWe can write any system of three equations with three variables in this form. Just to check that the above equation is true, let's multiply out the left-hand side to see if it equals the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\mathbf{x} & =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{blue}{3}} & {\\color{blue}{2}} & {\\color{blue}{-2}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{2}} & {\\color{blue}{-1}} & {\\color{blue}{-4}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{2}} & {\\color{blue}{1}} & {\\color{blue}{-3}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{blue}{3}}x+{\\color{blue}{2}}y\\text{ }{\\color{blue}{-}}\\text{ }{\\color{blue}{2}}z \\\\ {\\color{blue}{2}}x\\text{ }{\\color{blue}{-}}\\text{ }{\\color{blue}{1}}y\\text{ }{\\color{blue}{-}}\\text{ }{\\color{blue}{4}}z \\\\ {\\color{blue}{2}}x+{\\color{blue}{1}}y\\text{ }{\\color{blue}{-}}\\text{ }{\\color{blue}{3}}z\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{red}{7}} \\\\ {\\color{red}{2}} \\\\ {\\color{red}{3}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{b}\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186061006 :tutorial/content "How do we find the determinant of a $3\\times3$ matrix such as\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$A=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nOne way to do this is to take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as shown below:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{red}{7}} & {\\color{red}{1}} & {\\color{red}{-3}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{3}} & {\\color{blue}{2}} & {\\color{blue}{4}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{5}} & {\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{blue}{-1}}\\end{vmatrix} & =\\underbrace{(\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{red}{7}}\\hspace{0.167em})}_{{\\color{red}{{a}_{11}}}}\\cdot \\overbrace{\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{blue}{2}} & {\\color{blue}{4}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{blue}{-1}}\\end{vmatrix}}^{{\\color{blue}{{M}_{11}}}}-\\underbrace{(\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{red}{1\\hspace{0.167em}}})}_{{\\color{red}{{a}_{12}}}}\\cdot \\overbrace{\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{blue}{3}} & {\\color{blue}{4}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{5}} & {\\color{blue}{-1}}\\end{vmatrix}}^{{\\color{blue}{{M}_{12}}}}+\\underbrace{({\\color{red}{-3}})}_{{\\color{red}{{a}_{13}}}}\\cdot \\overbrace{\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{blue}{3}} & {\\color{blue}{2}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{5}} & {\\color{blue}{0}}\\end{vmatrix}}^{{\\color{blue}{{M}_{13}}}} \\\\  & =7(-2-0)-1(-3-20)-3(0-10) \\\\  & =-14+23+30 \\\\  & =39\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the above, we have denoted $\\color{blue}M_{1j}$ as the minor of the entry ${\\color{red}a_{1j}}.$\n\nIn general, the determinant of a $3\\times 3$ matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\det(A) = {\\color{red}a_{11}}{\\color{blue}M_{11}} - {\\color{red}a_{12}}{\\color{blue}M_{12}} + {\\color{red}a_{13}}{\\color{blue}M_{13}}.\n$$\n\nThat is, if $A=\\begin{bmatrix}a & b & c \\\\ d & e & f \\\\ g & h & i\\end{bmatrix},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}a & b & c \\\\ d & e & f \\\\ g & h & i\\end{vmatrix} & =a\\begin{vmatrix}e & f \\\\ h & i\\end{vmatrix}-b\\begin{vmatrix}d & f \\\\ g & i\\end{vmatrix}+c\\begin{vmatrix}d & e \\\\ g & h\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =a(ei-fh)-b(di-fg)+c(dh-eg).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Important:** Do not forget about the *minus sign* in front of the *second term* in the formula!"] [17592186116497 :tutorial/content "To compute the product of an elementary matrix and another matrix, one option is to perform the matrix multiplication as usual. However, there's an easier, quicker way.\n\nRemember that every elementary matrix is the result of applying an elementary row operation to the identity matrix. If we multiply another matrix by an elementary matrix, we're effectively applying the elementary row operation to the other matrix.\n\nFor example, suppose we are asked to find the product $EA$ where\n$$\nE = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{array} \\right]} \\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad A = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}{\\color{red}3} & {\\color{red}4} \\\\ {\\color{blue}8} & {\\color{blue}6}\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nThe elementary matrix $E$ comes from swapping the two rows of the identity matrix ($R_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2$). So, to compute the product $EA,$ all we have to do is swap the two rows of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n$$\nEA = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}{\\color{blue}8} & {\\color{blue}6} \\\\ {\\color{red}3} & {\\color{red}4}\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nThis may seem almost too easy. But it's true! And we can verify it by computing the product the long way, using matrix multiplication. Indeed, we get the same result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}EA & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 8 & 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{blue}{8}} & {\\color{blue}{6}} \\\\ {\\color{red}{3}} & {\\color{red}{4}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we say that $E \\cdot A$ is equivalent to applying to $A$ the elementary row operation $R_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2.$\n\n**Watch out!** It's important to multiply $A$ from the *left*. If we multiply $A$ by an elementary matrix from the *right*, we will perform a *column* operation on $A$ (not a row operation). In this case, multiplying $A$ by $E$ from the right will have the effect of switching the *columns* of $A,$ not the rows.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AE & =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{red}{3}} & {\\color{blue}{4}} \\\\ {\\color{red}{8}} & {\\color{blue}{6}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{blue}{4}} & {\\color{red}{3}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{6}} & {\\color{red}{8}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186119593 :question/worked-solution "Given an invertible $n\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have\n\n$$\n|A^{-1}| = \\dfrac{1}{|A|}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $|A|$ using a Laplace expansion across the $3$rd row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A| & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 14 & -1 \\\\ 7 & -6 & -6 \\\\ {\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{blue}{-3}} & {\\color{blue}{0}}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-3(-1)^{3+2}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 7 & -6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-12+7) \\\\  & =-15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\det(A^{-1}) =  \\dfrac{1}{|A|} = -\\dfrac{1}{15}.\n$$"] [17592186119594 :question/worked-solution "We \"factor out\" the given determinant from the determinant we would like to find:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}a & b & c+1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{red}{a}}+{\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{red}{b}}+{\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{red}{c}}+{\\color{blue}{1}} \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the linearity property of determinants and expanding the second determinant across the $1$st row, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{red}{a}}+{\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{red}{b}}+{\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{red}{c}}+{\\color{blue}{1}} \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & =\\underbrace{\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{red}{a}} & {\\color{red}{b}} & {\\color{red}{c}} \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}}_{-10}+\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{blue}{1}} \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-10+1(-1)^{1+3}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-10-1 \\\\  & =-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186119690 :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{blue}{1}} & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ {\\color{red}{2}} & -1 & 5 & -7 \\\\ {\\color{red}{4}} & -2 & 4 & -5 \\\\ {\\color{red}{-3}} & 3 & -3 & -4\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Find a $3\\times 3$ determinant that has the same value.\n\n*Hint: Use elementary row operations to make the entries below $a_{11}$ equal to zero.*"] [17592186119691 :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ {\\color{red}{-1}} & {\\color{red}{2}} & {\\color{blue}{1}} & {\\color{red}{-3}} \\\\ 2 & -2 & -2 & 4 \\\\ -4 & -4 & 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Find a $3\\times 3$ determinant that has the same value.\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the left and to the right of $a_{23}$ equal to zero.*"] [17592186120435 :question/worked-solution "We \"factor out\" the given determinant from the determinant we would like to find:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}a+2 & -5 \\\\ b+4 & 6\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{red}{(a+3)}}-{\\color{blue}{1}} & -5 \\\\ {\\color{red}{b}}+{\\color{blue}{4}} & 6\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the linearity property of determinants, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{red}{(a+3)}}-{\\color{blue}{1}} & -5 \\\\ {\\color{red}{b}}+{\\color{blue}{4}} & 6\\end{vmatrix} & =\\underbrace{\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{red}{a+3}} & -5 \\\\ {\\color{red}{b}} & 6\\end{vmatrix}}_{-7}+\\begin{vmatrix}-{\\color{blue}{1}} & -5 \\\\ {\\color{blue}{4}} & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-7+(-6+20) \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186120457 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the $1$st row of our determinant is almost equal to the $3$rd row. So, we can rewrite the given determinant, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}107 & 215 & 99 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 11 \\\\ 107 & 215 & 100\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{red}{107}} & {\\color{red}{215}} & {\\color{red}{99}} \\\\ 6 & 8 & 11 \\\\ {\\color{red}{107}}+{\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{red}{215}}+{\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{red}{99}}+{\\color{blue}{1}}\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, using the linearity property of determinants, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{red}{107}} & {\\color{red}{215}} & {\\color{red}{99}} \\\\ 6 & 8 & 11 \\\\ {\\color{red}{107}}+{\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{red}{215}}+{\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{red}{99}}+{\\color{blue}{1}}\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{red}{107}} & {\\color{red}{215}} & {\\color{red}{99}} \\\\ 6 & 8 & 11 \\\\ {\\color{red}{107}} & {\\color{red}{215}} & {\\color{red}{99}}\\end{vmatrix}+\\begin{vmatrix}107 & 215 & 99 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 11 \\\\ {\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{blue}{1}}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first determinant must be zero since it has two identical rows, while the second determinant can be expanded across the $3$rd row, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{red}{107}} & {\\color{red}{215}} & {\\color{red}{99}} \\\\ 6 & 8 & 11 \\\\ {\\color{red}{107}} & {\\color{red}{215}} & {\\color{red}{99}}\\end{vmatrix}+\\begin{vmatrix}107 & 215 & 99 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 11 \\\\ {\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{blue}{1}}\\end{vmatrix} & =0+1(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}107 & 215 \\\\ 6 & 8\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =107\\cdot 8-215\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =-434\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186120545 :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 & -7 & 10 \\\\ -9 & -1 & -6 & 9 \\\\ {\\color{red}{-2}} & {\\color{blue}{2}} & {\\color{red}{2}} & {\\color{red}{-6}} \\\\ 2 & 1 & 4 & -7\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the left and to the right of ${a}_{32}$ equal to zero.*"] [17592186120546 :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -2 & -3 & {\\color{blue}{3}} \\\\ -3 & -4 & -2 & {\\color{red}{12}} \\\\ -7 & 2 & 3 & {\\color{red}{-6}} \\\\ 1 & 3 & -2 & {\\color{red}{-3}}\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary row operations to make the entries below ${a}_{14}$ equal to zero.*"] [17592186120547 :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -4 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ {\\color{red}{6}} & {\\color{red}{-4}} & {\\color{blue}{2}} & {\\color{red}{2}} \\\\ 2 & -3 & -2 & -3\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the left and to the right of ${a}_{33}$ equal to zero.*"] [17592186121024 :tutorial/content "One final important property of the determinant is that it is **linear in each row and column**. For rows, for example, this means \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\det\\begin{bmatrix}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ {{\\color{red}{◻}}}_{1}+{{\\color{blue}{△}}}_{1} & {{\\color{red}{◻}}}_{2}+{{\\color{blue}{△}}}_{2} & … & {{\\color{red}{◻}}}_{n}+{{\\color{blue}{△}}}_{n} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\hspace{2em}\\hspace{2em}=\\det\\begin{bmatrix}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ {{\\color{red}{◻}}}_{1} & {{\\color{red}{◻}}}_{2} & … & {{\\color{red}{◻}}}_{n} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{bmatrix}+\\det\\begin{bmatrix}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ {{\\color{blue}{△}}}_{1} & {{\\color{blue}{△}}}_{2} & … & {{\\color{blue}{△}}}_{n} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor instance, it's easy to check that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{-2} & =det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ {\\color{red}{0}}+{\\color{blue}{3}} & {\\color{red}{4}}+{\\color{blue}{0}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ {\\color{red}{0}} & {\\color{red}{4}}\\end{bmatrix}}_{4}+\\underbrace{det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ {\\color{blue}{3}} & {\\color{blue}{0}}\\end{bmatrix}}_{-6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that this property also holds for columns:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det\\begin{bmatrix}⋯ & {{\\color{red}{◻}}}_{1}+{{\\color{blue}{△}}}_{1} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋯ & {{\\color{red}{◻}}}_{2}+{{\\color{blue}{△}}}_{2} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ ⋯ & {{\\color{red}{◻}}}_{n}+{{\\color{blue}{△}}}_{n} & ⋯\\end{bmatrix}=det\\begin{bmatrix}⋯ & {{\\color{red}{◻}}}_{1} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋯ & {{\\color{red}{◻}}}_{2} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ ⋯ & {{\\color{red}{◻}}}_{n} & ⋯\\end{bmatrix}+det\\begin{bmatrix}⋯ & {{\\color{blue}{△}}}_{1} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋯ & {{\\color{blue}{△}}}_{2} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ ⋯ & {{\\color{blue}{△}}}_{n} & ⋯\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{-2} & =det\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{red}{1}}+{\\color{blue}{0}} & 2 \\\\ {\\color{red}{0}}+{\\color{blue}{3}} & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{det\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{red}{1}} & 2 \\\\ {\\color{red}{0}} & 4\\end{bmatrix}}_{4}+\\underbrace{det\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{blue}{0}} & 2 \\\\ {\\color{blue}{3}} & 4\\end{bmatrix}}_{-6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186265857 :question/worked-solution "Here, $S$ is $3\\times 1$ matrix and $T$ is a $2\\times 1$ matrix. \n\nThere is $1$ column in $S$ and $2$ rows in $T$, so the product is undefined:\n\n$$\n{S}_{3\\times {\\color{red}{1}}}{\\times }_{{\\color{red}{\\ne }}}{T}_{{\\color{red}{2}}\\times 1}={\\color{red}{\\text{undefined}}}\n$$"] [17592186265950 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the $1$st row of our determinant is almost equal to the $3$rd row. So, we can rewrite the given determinant, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}160 & 184 & 76 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -8 \\\\ 160 & 184 & 78\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{red}{160}} & {\\color{red}{184}} & {\\color{red}{76}} \\\\ 3 & 4 & -8 \\\\ {\\color{red}{160}}+{\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{red}{184}}+{\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{red}{76}}+{\\color{blue}{2}}\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, using the linearity property of determinants, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{red}{160}} & {\\color{red}{184}} & {\\color{red}{76}} \\\\ 3 & 4 & -8 \\\\ {\\color{red}{160}}+{\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{red}{184}}+{\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{red}{76}}+{\\color{blue}{2}}\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{red}{160}} & {\\color{red}{184}} & {\\color{red}{76}} \\\\ 3 & 4 & -8 \\\\ {\\color{red}{160}} & {\\color{red}{184}} & {\\color{red}{76}}\\end{vmatrix}+\\begin{vmatrix}160 & 184 & 76 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -8 \\\\ {\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{blue}{2}}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first determinant must be zero since it has two identical rows, while the second determinant can be expanded across the $3$rd row, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{red}{160}} & {\\color{red}{184}} & {\\color{red}{76}} \\\\ 3 & 4 & -8 \\\\ {\\color{red}{160}} & {\\color{red}{184}} & {\\color{red}{76}}\\end{vmatrix}+\\begin{vmatrix}160 & 184 & 76 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -8 \\\\ {\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{blue}{2}}\\end{vmatrix} & =0+2(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}160 & 184 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (160\\cdot 4-184\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 88 \\\\  & =176\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186265974 :question/worked-solution "We \"factor out\" the given determinant from the determinant we would like to find:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ a+3 & b\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ {\\color{red}{a}}+{\\color{blue}{3}} & {\\color{red}{(b+2)}}-{\\color{blue}{2}}\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the linearity property of determinants, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ {\\color{red}{a}}+{\\color{blue}{3}} & {\\color{red}{(b+2)}}-{\\color{blue}{2}}\\end{vmatrix} & =\\underbrace{\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ {\\color{red}{a}} & {\\color{red}{b+2}}\\end{vmatrix}}_{8}+\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ {\\color{blue}{3}} & {\\color{blue}{-2}}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =8+(-6+6) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186266066 :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -3 & 4 & {\\color{red}{-6}} \\\\ 8 & -3 & 5 & {\\color{red}{3}} \\\\ -2 & 4 & -1 & {\\color{blue}{3}} \\\\ -5 & -2 & 1 & {\\color{red}{-6}}\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary row operations to make the entries above and below ${a}_{34}$ equal to zero.*"] [17592186266127 :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{red}{-6}} & {\\color{red}{9}} & {\\color{red}{-3}} & {\\color{blue}{3}} \\\\ 2 & -4 & 5 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -5 & -3 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 10 & -4 & 5\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the left of ${a}_{14}$ equal to zero.*"] [17592186354636 :question/worked-solution "We \"factor out\" the given determinant from the determinant we would like to find:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}a+1 & b & c \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{red}{a}}+{\\color{blue}{1}} & {\\color{red}{b}}+{\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{red}{c}}+{\\color{blue}{0}} \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the linearity property of determinants and expanding the second determinant across the $1$st row, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{red}{a}}+{\\color{blue}{1}} & {\\color{red}{b}}+{\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{red}{c}}+{\\color{blue}{0}} \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & =\\underbrace{\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{red}{a}} & {\\color{red}{b}} & {\\color{red}{c}} \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}}_{4}+\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{blue}{1}} & {\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{blue}{0}} \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4+1(-1)^{1+1}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4+1 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186354637 :question/worked-solution "We \"factor out\" the given determinant from the determinant we would like to find:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}a+1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ b & 4 & 2 \\\\ c & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{red}{(a+2)}}-{\\color{blue}{1}} & -2 & 2 \\\\ {\\color{red}{(b+3)}}-{\\color{blue}{3}} & 4 & 2 \\\\ {\\color{red}{c}}+{\\color{blue}{0}} & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNow, using the linearity property of determinants and expanding the second determinant across the $3$rd row, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{red}{(a+2)}}-{\\color{blue}{1}} & -2 & 2 \\\\ {\\color{red}{(b+3)}}-{\\color{blue}{3}} & 4 & 2 \\\\ {\\color{red}{c}}+{\\color{blue}{0}} & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & =\\underbrace{\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{red}{(a+2)}} & -2 & 2 \\\\ {\\color{red}{(b+3)}} & 4 & 2 \\\\ {\\color{red}{c}} & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}}_{5}+\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ {\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{blue}{1}}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =5+1\\cdot (-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =5+(-10) \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186354639 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the $2$nd row of our determinant is almost equal to the $3$rd row. So, we can rewrite the given determinant, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 91 & 92 & 93 \\\\ 90 & 92 & 93\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 & 6 \\\\ {\\color{red}{90}}+{\\color{blue}{1}} & {\\color{red}{92}}+{\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{red}{93}}+{\\color{blue}{0}} \\\\ {\\color{red}{90}} & {\\color{red}{92}} & {\\color{red}{93}}\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, using the linearity property of determinants, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 & 6 \\\\ {\\color{red}{90}}+{\\color{blue}{1}} & {\\color{red}{92}}+{\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{red}{93}}+{\\color{blue}{0}} \\\\ {\\color{red}{90}} & {\\color{red}{92}} & {\\color{red}{93}}\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 & 6 \\\\ {\\color{red}{90}} & {\\color{red}{92}} & {\\color{red}{93}} \\\\ {\\color{red}{90}} & {\\color{red}{92}} & {\\color{red}{93}}\\end{vmatrix}+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 & 6 \\\\ {\\color{blue}{1}} & {\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{blue}{0}} \\\\ {\\color{red}{90}} & {\\color{red}{92}} & {\\color{red}{93}}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first determinant must be zero since it has two identical rows, while the second determinant can be expanded across the $2$nd row, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 & 6 \\\\ {\\color{red}{90}} & {\\color{red}{92}} & {\\color{red}{93}} \\\\ {\\color{red}{90}} & {\\color{red}{92}} & {\\color{red}{93}}\\end{vmatrix}+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 & 6 \\\\ {\\color{blue}{1}} & {\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{blue}{0}} \\\\ {\\color{red}{90}} & {\\color{red}{92}} & {\\color{red}{93}}\\end{vmatrix} & =0+1(-1)^{2+1}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 6 \\\\ 92 & 93\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(3\\cdot 93-6\\cdot 92) \\\\  & =-(279-552) \\\\  & =273\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186354640 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the $1$st row of our determinant is almost equal to the $3$rd row. So, we can rewrite the given determinant, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}97 & 98 & 99 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 96 & 98 & 99\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{red}{96}}+{\\color{blue}{1}} & {\\color{red}{98}}+{\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{red}{99}}+{\\color{blue}{0}} \\\\ 6 & 4 & 2 \\\\ {\\color{red}{96}} & {\\color{red}{98}} & {\\color{red}{99}}\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, using the linearity property of determinants, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{red}{96}}+{\\color{blue}{1}} & {\\color{red}{98}}+{\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{red}{99}}+{\\color{blue}{0}} \\\\ 6 & 4 & 2 \\\\ {\\color{red}{96}} & {\\color{red}{98}} & {\\color{red}{99}}\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{red}{96}} & {\\color{red}{98}} & {\\color{red}{99}} \\\\ 6 & 4 & 2 \\\\ {\\color{red}{96}} & {\\color{red}{98}} & {\\color{red}{99}}\\end{vmatrix}+\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{blue}{1}} & {\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{blue}{0}} \\\\ 6 & 4 & 2 \\\\ {\\color{red}{96}} & {\\color{red}{98}} & {\\color{red}{99}}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first determinant must be zero since it has two identical rows, while the second determinant can be expanded across the $1$st row, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{red}{96}} & {\\color{red}{98}} & {\\color{red}{99}} \\\\ 6 & 4 & 2 \\\\ {\\color{red}{96}} & {\\color{red}{98}} & {\\color{red}{99}}\\end{vmatrix}+\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{blue}{1}} & {\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{blue}{0}} \\\\ 6 & 4 & 2 \\\\ {\\color{red}{96}} & {\\color{red}{98}} & {\\color{red}{99}}\\end{vmatrix} & =0+1(-1)^{1+1}\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 98 & 99\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 99-2\\cdot 98 \\\\  & =396-196 \\\\  & =200\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186357503 :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 2 & {\\color{red}{4}} & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & {\\color{blue}{-2}} & 5 \\\\ 3 & 5 & {\\color{red}{8}} & 6 \\\\ 4 & 3 & {\\color{red}{2}} & 5\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary row operations to make the entries above and below ${a}_{23}$ equal to zero.*"] [17592186357508 :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 & 6 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 7 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ {\\color{red}{2}} & {\\color{red}{-4}} & {\\color{blue}{-1}} & {\\color{red}{-3}}\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the left and to the right of ${a}_{43}$ equal to zero.*"] [17592186357512 :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ {\\color{red}{-2}} & {\\color{blue}{1}} & {\\color{red}{-3}} & {\\color{red}{4}} \\\\ 1 & 2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 2 & 5\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the left and to the right of ${a}_{22}$ equal to zero.*"] [17592186357513 :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 & -3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ {\\color{red}{4}} & {\\color{red}{-2}} & {\\color{red}{3}} & {\\color{blue}{1}}\\end{vmatrix}\n$